+## When everyone can't be on one WiFi
+
+Campus networks often isolate clients from each other, cap the guest SSID, or
+put phones and laptops on separate VLANs — so `192.168.x.x:3000` is unreachable
+even when every device shows full bars. Nothing in the game actually requires a
+LAN: the client connects to whatever origin served it, so all that's needed is a
+public address pointing at the host laptop.
+
+### Option A — Cloudflare Tunnel (recommended)
+
+Outbound-only, so it needs no port forwarding, no router access and no admin
+rights on the network. Install once:
+
+```
+winget install --id Cloudflare.cloudflared
+```
+
+Start the game as usual with `.\start.cmd`, then in a second terminal:
+
+```
+cloudflared tunnel --url http://localhost:3000
+```
+
+It prints an `https://<random-words>.trycloudflare.com` address. Open the
+dashboard on that URL rather than `localhost`, and the QR code and join link
+will point at it automatically. Players can then be on campus WiFi, mobile
+data, or at home.
+
+Two things this buys beyond reach: HTTPS, so phones don't warn on the join page;
+and a player whose phone falls off WiFi onto mobile data stays connected instead
+of dropping out of the round.
+
+The quick-tunnel URL changes every run, which is fine for a game night — the QR
+code is regenerated from it each time. A named tunnel on a Cloudflare account
+gives a stable hostname if you'd rather have one.
+
+### Option B — deploy it
+
+Any Node host with a free tier works (Render, Railway, Fly). Build command
+`npm install`, start command `npm start`; the platform supplies `PORT`. Then
+there's no host laptop in the loop and the URL is permanent, which suits a
+recurring club event.
+
+State is in memory, so three things follow:
+
+- **Run exactly one instance.** Two instances are two disjoint game worlds, and
+  players land in whichever one the load balancer picks.
+- **Free tiers sleep when idle.** Hit the URL a few minutes before you start so
+  the cold boot isn't happening mid-lobby. Traffic during a round keeps it up.
+- **A restart wipes the session**, the same as losing the laptop would. Fine for
+  one night; just don't redeploy mid-game.
+
+Set `PUBLIC_URL` to the deployed address (`https://your-app.onrender.com`) so
+the join link and QR read correctly.
+
+### How the join URL is chosen
+
+`/api/network` picks, in order:
+
+1. `PUBLIC_URL`, if set — always wins, for a known fixed address.
+2. The `X-Forwarded-Proto` / `X-Forwarded-Host` pair, when the request came
+   through a tunnel or a platform proxy.
+3. Otherwise the host's first non-internal IPv4 and `PORT` — the LAN behaviour,
+   unchanged.
+
+### One caveat
+
+A public address means anyone with the link reaches the lobby. Joining still
+needs the 4-character code — roughly a million combinations, with ambiguous
+characters left out — so nobody stumbles in by accident, but that code is the
+only gate and the host can remove strays from the roster. Worth not posting the
+tunnel URL anywhere public before the event.
+
+---
